    "notes": [
      "Same inputs as the article (ORE Example_9 files, libor configuration, 240 x 1M curves grid): ORE 1,609,885.84 vs QuantLib 1,608,800.94, gap 1,084.89 EUR = 1.08 bp.",
      "Schedules agree exactly with ORE's cashflow report: 20 fixed and 40 float payment dates, accrual dates, fixing dates and accrual fractions - both processes share QuantLib's TARGET calendar, 30/360 and A360.",
      "The gap is not mainly interpolation: 1,079.75 EUR (99.5%) comes from extrapolating past the last grid date 2036-02-05 to the final payment 2036-03-03 (27 days); interpolation inside the grid accounts for 5.17 EUR.",
      "The in-grid residual (-5.18 EUR, QuantLib - ORE) is the fixed leg: the 8 fixed coupons paid in a grid month that holds a native pillar (-5.18 EUR; DF errors -8.0e-06 to +1.2e-06). The float coupons ending in those months are off by up to 79.68 EUR each, but single-curve par coupons telescope (float leg = N x (DF(first start) - DF(last end))), so each DF error returns with the opposite sign in the next coupon: the 9 pairs net to -8.6e-09 EUR (largest pair 3.3e-09); all other in-grid cashflows +4.1e-09 EUR. Rebuild check: inserting the native pillars into the grid moves QuantLib by +5.18 EUR (grid_plus_native_pillars vs grid_plus_native_tail).",
      "Smoother interpolation on the same grid moves the price by only +7.05 EUR (monotonic log-cubic) / +7.04 EUR (natural log-cubic): ORE's curve is itself LogLinear in DF, so LogLinear is already the right scheme.",
      "Over 2036-02-05 -> 2036-03-03 QuantLib extrapolates a 1.550% forward (continuous A365) where ORE's curve implies 1.370%.",
      "Away from native-pillar months and the grid end, cashflow PVs agree to 2.2e-09 EUR."
    ]
  }
